  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Language, in the product
  //
  // The product carries no locale in its URLs, because `robots.ts` disallows
  // every path and there is no crawler behind a login to need one. So the
  // language is chosen once during onboarding and changed afterwards in account
  // settings, and it lives on the identity rather than on the browser, which is
  // what makes it follow a person to a new laptop. ADR-0022.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "account.language.heading": "Language",
  "account.language.body": "This is stored against your account rather than this browser, so it follows you to a new device. It changes what {brand} says to you and nothing about what it does.",
  "onboarding.language.heading": "Is this the right language?",
  "onboarding.language.body": "We guessed from your browser. Nothing is stored until you answer, and you can change it later in your account.",
  "onboarding.language.confirm": "Yes, continue",
  "onboarding.language.confirmed": "Language saved. You can change it in your account at any time.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Onboarding: the six screens between signing up and having a company.
  //
  // WHY THE COPY IS HERE AND THE REST OF apps/app IS NOT, since that is a visible
  // inconsistency and an honest one. CLAUDE.md §5b says every user-visible string
  // is a key. In `apps/app` that has been true of two surfaces and nothing else;
  // the other fifty files hardcode English, and no lint rule refuses them. This
  // flow is catalogued because it is the FIRST thing a customer reads, and
  // because the operator chose full translation for it on 2026-08-21. The rest of
  // the app is debt, and it is written down as debt rather than implied to be
  // finished.
  //
  // VOICE: declarative, concrete, unhedged. No em dash reaches any of these, in
  // any language (§5a), and `noEmDash` covers this file.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The two controls every screen shares.
  "onboarding.back": "Back",
  "onboarding.continue": "Continue",
  // The stepper's accessible name. A progress element would announce a
  // percentage; what a person wants read to them is an ordinal position.
  "onboarding.progress": "Step {step} of {total}",
  // 0 — Welcome. No input, and that is deliberate: the first screen states what
  // the next minute is for rather than asking for something.
  // 1 — Language. `onboarding.language.heading` and `.body` are declared further
  // up, beside the account setting they share wording with. This is the third
  // state: the language was already chosen, so there is nothing to confirm.
  // 1 — Personalization. The name and the language on one screen, because both
  // are facts about the PERSON and asking them separately makes two screens out
  // of one question.
